{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015vQr22CdQCXVCXCvEeUeJm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-572\"\n3\ttitle = \"The approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk: lanes author Drafted rows, a first-approval adjudication flips and snapshots them\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tneeds = [\"WI-571\"]\n6\tspecref = \"\"\n7\tbuildtier = \"strong\"\n8\tpriority = 5\n9\tsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n10\t+++\n11\t\n12\t## Deliverable\n13\t\n14\tThe approval act on a spine row — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded`\n15\tand the `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is now the\n16\tadjudicator's, on the serial trunk side. A worker lane authors `Drafted` rows\n17\tand amends cell text; nothing else changed about what a lane may do.\n18\t\n19\t1. **Lanes cannot approve.** The merge slot REFUSES a work branch whose spine\n20\t   delta flips a `Status`, mints a row born claiming approval, or writes the\n21\t   snapshot directory — by name, listing each row, its registry and every file\n22\t   written, with the remedy. Construction-first: `staged_approval_acts` reports\n23\t   exactly the set `staged_spine_amendments` exempts, off one shared two-tree\n24\t   walk (`_spine_row_sides`), so no second detector entered. The judgement is\n25\t   `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal`; `integrate._approval_act_refusal`\n26\t   is the rung. Verified against the record: the reader reproduces the\n27\t   pre-ruling census exactly — the four flips at `580df781` and the\n28\t   born-`Approved` rows of WI-483, WI-500, WI-501, WI-507.\n29\t2. **A first-approval adjudication arm exists.** Trigger (a2) mints ONE\n30\t   `brief = \"first-approval\"` row per merge over the `Drafted` rows the lane\n31\t   handed over, on the rungs the dial releases; a held rung is not minted and\n32\t   surfaces to the owner as before. Its brief renders each row's WHOLE chain\n33\t   (`trace.spine_chain`, extracted from `reattest_model` so the judge and the\n34\t   owner's surface cannot disagree) and asks one question — approve, or return\n35\t   with findings — with the `--approves` argument of the approving commit\n36\t   derived rather than typed. Its verdict grammar is its own. The adjudication\n37\t   lane is exempt from rung 1, and it already runs ALONE\n38\t   (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), which is the concurrency guarantee the ruling\n39\t   points the act at rather than a new mechanism.\n40\t3. **The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The stale \"the flip is\n41\t   the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" — false since OI-45 (b) retired that\n42\t   tool — is replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot that reads the declared\n43\t   gate authority for the tiers shown and tells the session whether the\n44\t   re-attestation is its own act or the owner's.\n45\t4. **The doctrine says it once:** PROCESS.md §4's fixed points, linking to\n46\t   PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the ruling, its two\n47\t   reasons, the division-of-labour table, the three holding mechanisms); OI-45\n48\t   (b) narrowed by one sentence; `gate-advance`, `spine-authoring` and\n49\t   `worker.template.md` updated.\n50\t5. **Tests** in the modules' existing style: five at the merge slot, four at the\n51\t   reader, three at the trigger, five at the brief.\n52\t\n53\tDeviations, the ratchet re-stamps, and the one follow-on this row deliberately\n54\tdoes not take (narrowing the owner's approval brief to the held rungs) are in\n55\t`docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`.\n56\t\n57\t## Context\n58\t\n59\tFiled 2026-09-01 (evening supervised session) from the owner's ruling made in session (docs/log.d/2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md, compiled): approval acts on spine rows - the Status flip and the anchoring snapshot - are the adjudicator's alone, on the serial trunk side, for whole-chain context and for concurrency. A lane's merge is refused on any flip, born-Approved row or snapshot write; a first-approval adjudication arm is minted at merge with the whole chain in its brief and flips + snapshots on trunk; the amendment arm's stale mechanical-tool line is replaced by the true aftermath; the doctrine is stated once. Serialized behind the copy-scope row (both touch intake.py and baseline_snapshot.py). Read the plan's section 2 before widening.\n60\t\n61\t**Standing constraint (the ruling this row executes):** if this row authors\n62\tor amends spine rows (SR/LLR/TC), leave them `Drafted`; do NOT flip any\n63\t`Status`, and do NOT run `intake.py snapshot` or write\n64\t`docs/archive/last_approved/` on this lane — drive the new refusal and the\n65\tadjudication arm on a scaffold. The first-approval adjudication this row\n66\tships is what performs the flip and the snapshot, on trunk.\n67\t\n68\tHonoured: this lane authored no spine row and moved no `Status`. Every arm is\n69\tdriven on a scaffold repo built by the tests.\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8876d3e3-9ead-4365-844a-04fd0bdeb9de","uuid":"84d5bfd1-59ca-45e0-a50a-3299c87e017c","timestamp":"2026-09-02T04:28:06.588Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-572-the-approval-act-is-the-adjudi/docs/work/active/wi-572-the-approval-act-is-the-adjudi/WI-572-the-approval-act-is-the-adjudi.md","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-572\"\ntitle = \"The approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk: lanes author Drafted rows, a first-approval adjudication flips and snapshots them\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nneeds = [\"WI-571\"]\nspecref = \"\"\nbuildtier = \"strong\"\npriority = 5\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n+++\n\n## Deliverable\n\nThe approval act on a spine row — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded`\nand the `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is now the\nadjudicator's, on the serial trunk side. A worker lane authors `Drafted` rows\nand amends cell text; nothing else changed about what a lane may do.\n\n1. **Lanes cannot approve.** The merge slot REFUSES a work branch whose spine\n   delta flips a `Status`, mints a row born claiming approval, or writes the\n   snapshot directory — by name, listing each row, its registry and every file\n   written, with the remedy. Construction-first: `staged_approval_acts` reports\n   exactly the set `staged_spine_amendments` exempts, off one shared two-tree\n   walk (`_spine_row_sides`), so no second detector entered. The judgement is\n   `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal`; `integrate._approval_act_refusal`\n   is the rung. Verified against the record: the reader reproduces the\n   pre-ruling census exactly — the four flips at `580df781` and the\n   born-`Approved` rows of WI-483, WI-500, WI-501, WI-507.\n2. **A first-approval adjudication arm exists.** Trigger (a2) mints ONE\n   `brief = \"first-approval\"` row per merge over the `Drafted` rows the lane\n   handed over, on the rungs the dial releases; a held rung is not minted and\n   surfaces to the owner as before. Its brief renders each row's WHOLE chain\n   (`trace.spine_chain`, extracted from `reattest_model` so the judge and the\n   owner's surface cannot disagree) and asks one question — approve, or return\n   with findings — with the `--approves` argument of the approving commit\n   derived rather than typed. Its verdict grammar is its own. The adjudication\n   lane is exempt from rung 1, and it already runs ALONE\n   (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), which is the concurrency guarantee the ruling\n   points the act at rather than a new mechanism.\n3. **The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The stale \"the flip is\n   the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" — false since OI-45 (b) retired that\n   tool — is replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot that reads the declared\n   gate authority for the tiers shown and tells the session whether the\n   re-attestation is its own act or the owner's.\n4. **The doctrine says it once:** PROCESS.md §4's fixed points, linking to\n   PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the ruling, its two\n   reasons, the division-of-labour table, the three holding mechanisms); OI-45\n   (b) narrowed by one sentence; `gate-advance`, `spine-authoring` and\n   `worker.template.md` updated.\n5. **Tests** in the modules' existing style: five at the merge slot, four at the\n   reader, three at the trigger, five at the brief.\n\nDeviations, the ratchet re-stamps, and the one follow-on this row deliberately\ndoes not take (narrowing the owner's approval brief to the held rungs) are in\n`docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`.\n\n## Context\n\nFiled 2026-09-01 (evening supervised session) from the owner's ruling made in session (docs/log.d/2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md, compiled): approval acts on spine rows - the Status flip and the anchoring snapshot - are the adjudicator's alone, on the serial trunk side, for whole-chain context and for concurrency. A lane's merge is refused on any flip, born-Approved row or snapshot write; a first-approval adjudication arm is minted at merge with the whole chain in its brief and flips + snapshots on trunk; the amendment arm's stale mechanical-tool line is replaced by the true aftermath; the doctrine is stated once. Serialized behind the copy-scope row (both touch intake.py and baseline_snapshot.py). Read the plan's section 2 before widening.\n\n**Standing constraint (the ruling this row executes):** if this row authors\nor amends spine rows (SR/LLR/TC), leave them `Drafted`; do NOT flip any\n`Status`, and do NOT run `intake.py snapshot` or write\n`docs/archive/last_approved/` on this lane — drive the new refusal and the\nadjudication arm on a scaffold. The first-approval adjudication this row\nships is what performs the flip and the snapshot, on trunk.\n\nHonoured: this lane authored no spine row and moved no `Status`. Every arm is\ndriven on a scaffold repo built by the tests.\n","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"to