{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J1sq2y8gk9VBT2ZGMxcB79","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## 2026-09-01 — WI-572: the approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk\n2\t\n3\t**Spec of record:** `../plans/2026-09-01-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`\n4\t(the owner's ruling, 2026-09-01, recorded in\n5\t`../log.md` from `2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md`). Serialized behind\n6\tWI-571 (the copy-scope row); both touch `intake.py` / `baseline_snapshot.py`.\n7\t\n8\t**In one line:** a worker lane may author `Drafted` spine rows and amend cell\n9\ttext, but the approval act — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded` and\n10\tthe `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is the adjudicator's,\n11\tperformed on the serial trunk side.\n12\t\n13\t### The baseline this row is measured against\n14\t\n15\tEvery commit that moved an `\"Approved\"` string in a spine registry before the\n16\truling, classified by where it happened:\n17\t\n18\t- **1 worker-lane flip** — `580df781` (WI-508 slice 6), whose next review round\n19\t  returned CHANGES-REQUESTED against exactly those flips.\n20\t- **4 lanes minted rows born `Approved`**, skipping the brief entirely —\n21\t  `8848f6fb` (WI-483), `ad2222df` (WI-500), `69e4a854` (WI-501), `0cfb2e6f`\n22\t  (WI-507).\n23\t- The rest were trunk sittings or the pre-ladder rename.\n24\t\n25\tfig: 17 commits, classified by subject; `git log --format='%h|%s'\n26\t-S'\"Approved\"' fd86e47f -- docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\n27\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml docs/test/test-cases.toml\n28\tdocs/requirements/system-requirements.csv\n29\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv docs/test/test-cases.csv`\n30\tat `fd86e47f`.\n31\t\n32\t### Deliverables\n33\t\n34\t**1. A lane's merge is refused when its delta performs an approval act.**\n35\t`acceptance_record.staged_approval_acts` reports a `Status` crossing INTO\n36\t`Approved`/`Founded` and a row that arrives already claiming one;\n37\t`lane_approval_refusal` words the refusal, naming each row, its registry, the\n38\tshape of act, and every `docs/archive/last_approved/` file the branch wrote.\n39\t`integrate._approval_act_refusal` is the rung, beside `_minted_id_refusal`\n40\twhose shape it copies exactly — the merge base, and the ladder placement.\n41\t\n42\tConstruction-first, as the plan required: `staged_spine_amendments` already\n43\tdiffs the merged commit and EXEMPTS a row whose Status moved; the new reader\n44\treports precisely that exempted set. All three readers (plus\n45\t`staged_drafted_rows`) now share ONE two-tree walk, `_spine_row_sides`,\n46\textracted rather than copied. Verified against the record itself: the reader\n47\treproduces the census above exactly — four flips at `580df781`, and the\n48\tborn-`Approved` rows of all four lanes.\n49\t\n50\tThe judgement lives in `acceptance_record`, not in the merge slot, on LLR-178's\n51\tseparation: the coordinator that merges is not the reader that decides what a\n52\tspine delta did. That also kept `integrate.py`'s size bump to a new rung's\n53\tirreducible core.\n54\t\n55\t**2. The first-approval adjudication arm.** If a lane may not approve what it\n56\tauthored, something must. `intake._first_approval_drafts` (trigger a2) mints ONE\n57\t`brief = \"first-approval\"` adjudication per merge over the `Drafted` rows the\n58\tdelta added or amended — the exact mirror of trigger (a), one section below it.\n59\t`APPROVAL_RUNG` names the DevStg rung each spine tier is approved into so the\n60\tdial can answer whether that tier is loop-held; a rung the dial HOLDS is not\n61\tminted (the owner approves those, through the approval brief, as today), and an\n62\tunmapped tier is held.\n63\t\n64\t`prompts/adjudicate-first-approval.template.md` is the fifth adjudicator brief,\n65\twith `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` behind it and its own verdict\n66\tgrammar (`OUTCOME: APPROVE|RETURN rows=N`). The brief renders each row's WHOLE\n67\tCHAIN, which is the owner's stated reason the act is the adjudicator's — so\n68\t`trace.reattest_model`'s `chain_of` closure became the public\n69\t`trace.spine_chain` + `chain_buckets`. The model's own `rows` list carries only\n70\twhat changed or is `Drafted`: the right answer to the re-attest brief's question\n71\tand exactly the wrong one here, where the settled parent and the passing sibling\n72\ttest ARE the evidence.\n73\t\n74\t`integrate._adjudication_lane` exempts the actor the ruling names. The\n75\tconcurrency half the owner asked for was **already built** and is not\n76\tre-implemented: an adjudication row is not `ordinary`, so\n77\t`dispatch._branch_exclusive` already runs its lane alone. The ruling is what\n78\tpoints the act at that guarantee.\n79\t\n80\t**3. The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The template's closing\n81\t\"the flip, if one is owed, is the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" went false\n82\twhen OI-45 (b) retired that tool, so a MEANING verdict on a loop-held rung ended\n83\tat a brief nobody was owed. Replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot\n84\t(`adjudicate_brief._aftermath`), which reads the declared gate authority for the\n85\ttiers actually shown and tells the session whether the re-attestation is its own\n86\tact or the owner's — rather than leaving it to read and interpret a dial\n87\tmid-verdict. `prompts/CATALOG.md` regenerated.\n88\t\n89\t**4. The doctrine says it once.** PROCESS.md §4 gains one clause in its fixed\n90\tpoints and links to PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the\n91\truling, its two reasons, the division-of-labour table the owner asked for in\n92\t§2a, and the three holding mechanisms). OI-45 (b) gains the narrowing sentence.\n93\t`gate-advance` names the acceptor its procedure was already addressed to;\n94\t`spine-authoring` opens with \"authoring is not approving\"; `worker.template.md`\n95\tgains the NEVER clause and its close ritual now says \"minted or amended\". The\n96\ttwo reference surfaces follow the mechanism they describe:\n97\t`docs/enforcement-audit.md` gains the rule's row (with its three-fold honest\n98\tresidue), and `docs/registry-machinery-reference.md` records the other half of\n99\tthe amendment walk, plus the narrowing on the `Founded` row's OI-45 citation.\n100\t\n101\t**5. Tests**, all in the modules' existing style: five at the merge slot, four\n102\tat the reader, three at the trigger, five at the brief.\n103\t\n104\t### Deviations from the plan\n105\t\n106\t- **The refusal points at PROCESS.md §4, not at the plan.** The plan's\n107\t  done-when 1 says the refusal \"points at this plan\". It is shipped kit code:\n108\t  a downstream repo has no `docs/plans/2026-09-01-...`, and CLAUDE.md's\n109\t  copy-ready rule refuses a token that cites a record the adopter can never\n110\t  read. PROCESS.md §4 ships, and now carries the ruling.\n111\t- **The adjudication runs as an exclusive claimed lane, not as a bare\n112\t  trunk-side session.** The plan says \"on the serial trunk side as an exclusive\n113\t  lane\". Read literally as \"commits directly on trunk\", that would be a new\n114\t  execution mode; read against this repo's vocabulary, an adjudication row is\n115\t  already claimed, already runs alone (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), and\n116\t  already merges through the serial fail-closed queue. So the ruling's\n117\t  substance — a work lane never approves, only an adjudication does, and two\n118\t  acts cannot overlap — is delivered by exempting the adjudication lane rather\n119\t  than by building a second path.\n120\t- **A bare withdrawal mints nothing.** A row moved `Approved` -> `Drafted` with\n121\t  no text change is not carried to the first-approval trigger: the lane's\n122\t  recorded call already answers it, and minting there would ask an adjudicator\n123\t  to undo a deliberate withdrawal. A withdrawn row whose text then moves DOES\n124\t  reach the surface; both halves are pinned.\n125\t- **One correction outside the row's own scope.** PROCESS.md §4's snapshot\n126\t  sentence still said the copy is \"replaced wholesale at each approval\", which\n127\t  WI-571 made false. It is the sentence this row was editing, so leaving a\n128\t  known-false clause in place was worse than the one-clause fix.\n129\t\n130\t### Ratchets re-stamped (each with its reason, in the commit that earned it)\n131\t\n132\t`integrate.py` 1,270 -> 1,298 (two stamps: the rung, then its exemption);\n133\t`intake.py` 1,179 -> 1,255 (trigger a2); `bootstrap.py` 1,652 -> 1,657 (the\n134\tMAPPING row); `trace.py:reattest_model` complexity DOWN 19 -> 13, recorded in\n135\tthe same commit as the extraction that earned it. Byte-watched:\n136\t`PROCESS.md` 87,871 -> 88,355, `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` 181,369 -> 185,060, and\n137\tbyte-budget-guard's own row to 4,906 (cap 5,000).\n138\t\n139\t### Not done here\n140\t\n141\tThe plan's §2a table row \"Surfaces to the owner\" says rows above the threshold\n142\tdo NOT surface to the owner. That is the *consequence* of this row, not a\n143\tseparate surface change: `trace.py --approve modified` still renders every\n144\t`Drafted` chain, held or released. Narrowing the owner's brief to the held rungs\n145\talone is a real follow-on and is deliberately not taken here — it would change\n146\twhat the owner sees at a sitting, which is the owner's call, not a side effect\n147\tof moving who acts.\n148\t\n149\tThe plan's §2a consequence — that the six MEANING rows of the WI-566 amendment\n150\tadjudication are this arm's first re-attestation case — is a trunk-side act on a\n151\tfuture adjudication, not something this lane may perform.\n152\t\n153\tDeferred open items: none — the ruling this row executes is already recorded,\n154\tand the owner-brief narrowing above is named here as a candidate follow-on\n155\trather than a decision owed back.\n156\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8876d3e3-9ead-4365-844a-04fd0bdeb9de","uuid":"82c63d2f-8dfa-4381-a9ff-701f118af347","timestamp":"2026-09-02T04:28:07.038Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-572-the-approval-act-is-the-adjudi/docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md","content":"## 2026-09-01 — WI-572: the approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk\n\n**Spec of record:** `../plans/2026-09-01-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`\n(the owner's ruling, 2026-09-01, recorded in\n`../log.md` from `2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md`). Serialized behind\nWI-571 (the copy-scope row); both touch `intake.py` / `baseline_snapshot.py`.\n\n**In one line:** a worker lane may author `Drafted` spine rows and amend cell\ntext, but the approval act — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded` and\nthe `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is the adjudicator's,\nperformed on the serial trunk side.\n\n### The baseline this row is measured against\n\nEvery commit that moved an `\"Approved\"` string in a spine registry before the\nruling, classified by where it happened:\n\n- **1 worker-lane flip** — `580df781` (WI-508 slice 6), whose next review round\n  returned CHANGES-REQUESTED against exactly those flips.\n- **4 lanes minted rows born `Approved`**, skipping the brief entirely —\n  `8848f6fb` (WI-483), `ad2222df` (WI-500), `69e4a854` (WI-501), `0cfb2e6f`\n  (WI-507).\n- The rest were trunk sittings or the pre-ladder rename.\n\nfig: 17 commits, classified by subject; `git log --format='%h|%s'\n-S'\"Approved\"' fd86e47f -- docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml docs/test/test-cases.toml\ndocs/requirements/system-requirements.csv\ndocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv docs/test/test-cases.csv`\nat `fd86e47f`.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n**1. A lane's merge is refused when its delta performs an approval act.**\n`acceptance_record.staged_approval_acts` reports a `Status` crossing INTO\n`Approved`/`Founded` and a row that arriv